#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063990 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc4294d6-3d60-5c9e-a862-863e1d129049" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d6501a20-6ebd-5786-870e-3527423b6332" :question/math-academy-id "e-449" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3}{5x+2}\\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, let\n\n$$\nu=5x+2\n$$\n\nThen differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 5 \\textrm d u\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$, solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{3}{5x + 2}dx &= 3\\int\\frac{1}{5x + 2}dx \\\\\n&= 3\\int\\frac{1}{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}\\int\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}u & + C \\\\ = \\frac{3}{5}\\ln & 5x + 2\\end{vmatrix}+ C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063991 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"}] :question/id #uuid "115b3fb1-1041-532a-b5fa-4d8a53c9ab2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-450" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\\textrm{d}x}$, writing your final answer as a single function." :question/worked-solution "First, let\n\n$$\nu=1+2x\n$$\n\nThen differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$, solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{1 + 2x}dx &= \\int\\frac{1}{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}u & + C \\\\ = \\frac{1}{2}\\ln & 1 + 2x\\end{vmatrix}+ C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac 1 2\\ln K\n$$\n\nfor another arbitrary constant $K > 0$, and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}1 + 2x & + C &= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln & 1 + 2x\\end{vmatrix}+ \\frac{1}{2}\\ln K \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln (K \\cdot \\mid 1 + 2x\\begin{vmatrix}) \\\\ = \\frac{1}{2}\\ln (K \\mid 1 + 2x\\end{vmatrix})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\ln \\left(K \\mid 1+2x \\mid \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063992 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af32f07f-fa65-5ede-a506-735dc2d3a5ef" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Combination of Linear Rational Functions Using the General Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "2c4912bd-290a-5074-b11b-459c77b7e238" :question/math-academy-id "e-451" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int\\left(\\dfrac{1}{1+2x} + \\dfrac{1}{1-2x}\\right)\\,\\textrm d x$." :question/worked-solution "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int(\\frac{1}{1 + 2x} + \\frac{1}{1 - 2x})dx &= \\int\\frac{1}{1 + 2x}dx + \\int\\frac{1}{1 - 2x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}1 + 2x & -\\frac{1}{2}\\ln & 1 - 2x\\end{vmatrix}+ C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid \\frac{1 + 2x}{1 - 2x}\\begin{vmatrix}+ \\frac{1}{2}\\ln K \\\\ = \\frac{1}{2}\\ln (K \\mid \\frac{1 + 2x}{1 - 2x}\\end{vmatrix})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cb2cf9c-0a11-5b33-b8de-390b92792c70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263888} {:answer/id #uuid "e44698f2-77ed-51cd-a304-5f4382988298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{|3x+4|}{K})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263889} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0fdccd-b602-5e49-9a18-3e6a4dad345a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263890} {:answer/id #uuid "8fdf8cb6-a386-5e3c-9e6e-1d5d842d0a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263891} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f1c2e7-8aad-540d-8588-145e7732a0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fdf8cb6-a386-5e3c-9e6e-1d5d842d0a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263891} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce6206fa-0296-5633-b6f6-5326aba01432" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263887}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2b73c30-360c-5ff5-b1c4-6d6aa37e868f" :question/math-academy-id "q-113533" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=3x+4.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52f16235-5464-5900-982b-bf5bebc413d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5x+3|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263895} {:answer/id #uuid "408a19d4-1a53-519c-a8a4-be6c298699e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5x+3|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263896} {:answer/id #uuid "3819a8fd-598d-5b1f-ba91-12d1f939a571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5x+3|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263897} {:answer/id #uuid "0db5fb6e-9dd5-5a6f-a713-4280839e5699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5x+3|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263898} {:answer/id #uuid "e96fa58a-4011-5594-86a3-87a075409f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5x+3|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0db5fb6e-9dd5-5a6f-a713-4280839e5699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5x+3|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263898} :answer-field/id #uuid "150eb0de-9569-57d4-9189-204884ef8829" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263894}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "148e2745-4d95-5c97-ae79-9141174f3198" :question/math-academy-id "q-113537" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4}{5x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=5x+3.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{5x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{5x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+3|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+3|+C & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+3|+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+3|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5x+3|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{4}{5x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|5x+3|).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b5f49ae-cdae-5bb9-8611-2370f3e8645f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{K}{|1-4x|})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263902} {:answer/id #uuid "afaca415-f89f-5dc7-9748-e70c1adf5470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-4x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263903} {:answer/id #uuid "695aaa94-6f83-5edc-96ca-ab870d0db9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-4x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263904} {:answer/id #uuid "b63aaaed-f8c7-5922-8061-ed1694f4d3d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-4x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263905} {:answer/id #uuid "0c8fc029-848c-55e8-9e45-eee733e043d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-4x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c8fc029-848c-55e8-9e45-eee733e043d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-4x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263906} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fb8989a-9843-54b5-b784-e3d5f647de17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263901}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7144c9d8-aace-59a9-b545-8002f1749a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-113538" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{1-4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=1-4x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{1-4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4x|+C & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4x|-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-4x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3}{1-4x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1-4x|).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af32f07f-fa65-5ede-a506-735dc2d3a5ef" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Combination of Linear Rational Functions Using the General Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91d73ed6-208d-5511-8f35-9d2f3479f0be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{3x+2}{3x-2}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263909} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac17f0e-436a-51f5-8b4e-ec9c08a4b51c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(3x+2)(3x-2)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263910} {:answer/id #uuid "3079fd7b-f36d-5e2a-bfe4-4bf229a24c4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(3x+2)(3x-2)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263911} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e7c119-2538-520d-85bb-19ce0176502a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{3x+2}{3x-2}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263912} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0f1b08-8841-5fe1-bcd2-f865c24de710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(3x+2)(3x-2)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd0f1b08-8841-5fe1-bcd2-f865c24de710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(3x+2)(3x-2)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263913} :answer-field/id #uuid "32dcfec7-7b31-5221-9306-3bc4841bd673" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263908}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e09b13a7-3a3e-5698-a973-d09dbf890472" :question/math-academy-id "q-113540" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{3x+2}+\\frac{1}{3x-2})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{3x+2}+\\frac{1}{3x-2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(3x+2)(3x-2)|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(3x+2)(3x-2)|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af32f07f-fa65-5ede-a506-735dc2d3a5ef" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Combination of Linear Rational Functions Using the General Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a02b7f38-ef2b-5f0c-b27e-c5033e9b8c32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(x+3)(x-3)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263916} {:answer/id #uuid "51438d68-c8ca-5463-8745-a657b136e92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(x+3)(x-3)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263917} {:answer/id #uuid "d654ccb5-6498-5578-885a-255d10371a3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(x+3)(x-3)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263918} {:answer/id #uuid "a07e2e9d-f1d6-5417-9ac8-4a49771bf319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(x+3)(x-3)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263919} {:answer/id #uuid "753170e7-af2f-5853-a179-bf2d9eb218d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(x+3)(x-3)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d654ccb5-6498-5578-885a-255d10371a3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(x+3)(x-3)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263918} :answer-field/id #uuid "50b575da-9b70-5615-b23f-285003fe99b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263915}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "48ad624f-bb7d-5d52-95c9-3147fbe15779" :question/math-academy-id "q-113544" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{x+3}+\\frac{1}{x-3})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{x+3}+\\frac{1}{x-3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+3)(x-3)|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(x+3)(x-3)|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc4294d6-3d60-5c9e-a862-863e1d129049" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "938d9ff8-864c-5d33-b70d-b6ee6bd040aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061301} {:answer/id #uuid "b38980f8-ef07-5145-ac55-096286ecbb17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061302} {:answer/id #uuid "b62b7065-dce3-5568-97b4-f71e75f4f1ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln \\mid x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061303} {:answer/id #uuid "049bf475-8b86-552c-86a0-91a29888bfef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061304} {:answer/id #uuid "1177d9e2-863f-5f99-953d-293bf6c24423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\ln \\mid x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b62b7065-dce3-5568-97b4-f71e75f4f1ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln \\mid x + 1 \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061303} :answer-field/id #uuid "96738ee0-5a89-513a-9fe2-ba75aebbfd4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061300}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fe299cc-e2f3-5032-a27f-6865f6c34c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-15707" :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{2}{x + 1}dx$?" :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=x+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b240a31-bbe0-50be-9398-ac1c24be5b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263923} {:answer/id #uuid "5610758c-288f-5760-8dee-bd20623fa045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263924} {:answer/id #uuid "0055d834-757f-59e3-a7dc-cfeb671ea6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263925} {:answer/id #uuid "d09a5cad-97ad-5539-a545-7c6dde319ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263926} {:answer/id #uuid "7389b19a-807b-55d1-bd05-4165f1a27d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5610758c-288f-5760-8dee-bd20623fa045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263924} :answer-field/id #uuid "616a6c30-5826-5ad3-bfb7-4e020555b25d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263922}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99292ea7-f528-52cb-8492-bc9d8ca80636" :question/math-academy-id "q-30368" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=3x+4.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+C & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+4|+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|3x+4|).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af32f07f-fa65-5ede-a506-735dc2d3a5ef" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Combination of Linear Rational Functions Using the General Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5661e0dc-f440-5000-9819-e69cd8e11ea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|(1+x)(1-x)|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263930} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2eafdf-b77a-507f-a90d-e3b8070c3dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{1+x}{1-x}|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263931} {:answer/id #uuid "0c8955d0-de70-5005-a156-5b8d02b925e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} 